          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "Yes, I10 is a complete code. It has three characters and does not need a fourth, fifth or sixth character. The code is for essential (primary) high blood pressure in the US version of ICD-10 (ICD-10-CM). Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.\n\nMost ICD-10-CM codes do need extra characters, so I10 is unusual. The same source notes that high blood pressure described as arterial, essential, primary or systemic all map to I10. Adding more characters to it is not possible.\n\nThe source also says the older benign-versus-malignant split for hypertension was not carried into this version, so there is no separate \"malignant hypertension\" code.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover how a clinician decides which code applies to a particular patient. They also do not explain the rules for hypertension that occurs together with heart or kidney disease. The block that holds I10 (I10 to I16) includes combination codes for those cases, according to *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99)*. For coding decisions, a trained medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tables should be consulted.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nI made one change. The earlier answer named the I11 to I13 codes, which the retrieved passages do not name, so I replaced that with a description the passages do support. The earlier answer already contained the disclaimer, and it appears here unchanged.",
